# WHAT THE LAMPS COST AT NIGHT (#56): the night village was the slowest web window
# (34 ms p50, tours/web-frames.tour) though it is among the cheapest on the desktop,
# and the Compatibility renderer the web draws with pays a pass per light per mesh.
# Each window splits the frame by its lamps inside one run (FoliagePerf `lamps`:
# every local light switched off what it lights; `lamp_shadows`: off what it casts),
# against the noise of the same switch thrown off twice, with the coast by day as
# the control. `perf lit` counts what the lamps reach (light-geometry pairs against
# the web renderer's lights-per-object cap), the same on any load. On the desktop's own renderer, and on the web's, on this machine:
#   tools/tour.sh tours/night-lamps.tour --seed=7 --weather=clear:0 --stats
#   godot --path . --fixed-fps 60 --rendering-method gl_compatibility -- --tour=tours/night-lamps.tour --seed=7 --weather=clear:0 --stats --quality=web
coast calm
hour 11
place coast
wait 2.0
perf stats begin
walk 1,0 2.0
walk -1,0 2.0
perf stats end coast_day
perf noise 8
perf lamps 8
shot 01-coast-day with land:coast

hour 23
near house
wait 2.0
perf stats begin
walk -1,0 2.0
walk 1,0 2.0
perf stats end village_night
perf lit
perf noise 8
perf lamps 8
perf lamp_shadows 8
perf props 8
perf terrain 8
shot 02-village-night

hour 19.5
near house
wait 1.5
spawn runner
spawn runner@90
spawn harvester@180
spawn cutter@270
wait 1.0
perf stats begin
wait 3.0
perf stats end crowd_dusk
perf lit
perf noise 8
perf lamps 8
perf lamp_shadows 8
shot 03-crowd-dusk with mob:runner,mob:harvester,mob:cutter
